    "description": "Specialist surgical travel combines an operation with assessment, anesthesia, recovery, and subsequent care across borders. Iranian research documents organizational challenges in serving international patients, but does not establish comparative surgical outcomes by hospital or specialty.",
    "tags": ["Specialist surgery", "Health travel", "Surgical safety", "Recovery", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:42:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:42:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Specialist Surgery in Iran: Safety and Continuity of Care",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to surgical travel, Iranian hospital service challenges, and the distinction between safety standards and verified outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An operation within a wider care pathway",
        "content": "International surgical care involves more than selecting an operation. The World Health Organization's surgical safety framework includes the work of surgeons, anesthesia professionals, and nurses, with checks before anesthesia, before incision, and before the patient leaves the operating room. It provides a useful way to understand the supporting processes behind a procedure. It does not establish that any particular Iranian hospital implements them. [s1]\n\nThis page therefore distinguishes internationally described safety practices from evidence about services in Iran. The inspected Iranian studies concern the organization of medical travel, rather than independently audited results for individual operations. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-process",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What surgical safety involves",
        "content": "WHO's checklist begins with confirmation of identity, procedure, site, and consent. It also addresses allergies, airway concerns, anesthesia equipment, and anticipated blood loss. Before incision, the team discusses critical events, confirms sterility, and reviews relevant imaging. Before departure from the operating room, checks include instrument counts, specimen labeling, and concerns for recovery. These are distinct responsibilities within a coordinated process, rather than a single assurance that an operation is safe. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Safety checks around an operation",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Before anesthesia\"] -->|\"Confirm\"| B[\"Identity, procedure, site and consent\"]\n  B -->|\"Proceed to next safety checkpoint\"| C[\"Before incision\"]\n  C -->|\"Team reviews\"| D[\"Critical events, sterility and imaging\"]\n  D -->|\"Proceed to final safety checkpoint\"| E[\"Before leaving operating room\"]\n  E -->|\"Team confirms\"| F[\"Counts, specimen labels and recovery concerns\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram summarizes selected WHO checklist functions in their procedural order. It is an international reference framework, not evidence of implementation at an Iranian facility.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iranian service organization and its limitations",
        "content": "A qualitative study published in Health Scope examined hospital challenges through interviews with people involved in Iranian hospitals and medical tourism. It identified problems involving governance, finance, staffing, equipment and medicines, information systems, and service delivery. The authors proposed improvements to International Patient Departments, hospital units concerned with serving patients from abroad, alongside insurance guidance and better information infrastructure. These proposals were not proof that the problems had subsequently been resolved. [s2]\n\nA separate study at Iran University of Medical Sciences reported concerns about communication, patient follow-up, and multilingual records. This is evidence about participants' experiences and organizational weaknesses within the study setting, not a complication rate or a verdict on every Iranian hospital. Together, the studies explain why coordination deserves attention alongside surgical expertise. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovery and the journey home",
        "content": "CDC guidance notes that surgery and air travel can increase blood-clot risk, while infection is an important complication of medical tourism. Recovery schedules therefore cannot be inferred from an advertised holiday itinerary. The guidance also emphasizes access to medical records and subsequent clinical care. [s4]\n\nThe evidence reviewed does not support ranking Iranian surgical centers or making generalized claims about success rates. A publication-ready specialty directory still requires verification of current services and procedure-specific outcomes.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
