    "description": "Rehabilitation supports functioning and participation in daily life after illness, injury, surgery, or changes associated with aging. Iranian research discusses referral, workforce, financing, and equipment barriers, but does not establish the availability or outcomes of rehabilitation programs for short-stay international patients.",
    "tags": ["Rehabilitation", "Physiotherapy", "Occupational therapy", "Accessibility", "Health travel", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:42:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:42:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Rehabilitation Services in Iran: Function, Access and Continuity",
      "description": "Explore rehabilitation disciplines, Iranian service-access research, and the evidence needed to assess rehabilitation across borders."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rehabilitation focuses on everyday functioning",
        "content": "Rehabilitation helps people function more independently in their own environments. WHO describes an approach that can combine treatment, assistive products, education, changes to tasks, and modifications to surroundings. Relevant goals include communicating, moving, eating, working, or participating in family life. It can be needed after surgery or injury, alongside a chronic condition, or as functioning changes with age. [s1]\n\nThis makes rehabilitation different from a generic promise of rest or wellness. Its purpose is tied to a person's functional needs and goals. In a travel context, the key editorial question is therefore what a service actually provides and how it connects with continuing care, rather than whether a destination advertises recovery in general. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "disciplines",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several professions can contribute",
        "content": "WHO identifies a multidisciplinary workforce that includes physiotherapists, occupational therapists, speech and language therapists, audiologists, rehabilitation physicians and nurses, and specialists in orthotics and prosthetics. Orthotics concerns supportive devices such as braces; prosthetics concerns replacement devices for missing body parts. Rehabilitation can take place in hospitals, outpatient practices, homes, schools, and workplaces. These are international descriptions, not confirmation that every Iranian center offers each service. [s1]\n\nResearch on integrating rehabilitation into Iran's primary health care system focused specifically on physiotherapy, occupational therapy, and prosthetics and orthotics. Its scope should not be mistaken for a complete assessment of every rehabilitation profession or patient group. The distinction matters when interpreting findings that are sometimes presented under the broad label of rehabilitation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-policy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access and coordination in Iran",
        "content": "A qualitative Iranian study published in 2022 examined policy options for connecting rehabilitation with primary health care, the community-facing part of a health system. Participants proposed clearer referral pathways, stronger teamwork, additional workforce training, improved financing, and better information systems. Access to equipment and materials, particularly for orthotic and prosthetic services, was another concern. These were proposed improvements, not a report that integration had been completed nationally. [s2]\n\nThe study also identified insufficient information about physical access to rehabilitation resources as a policy problem. It cannot establish that a service is available to a foreign visitor simply because that service exists somewhere in Iran. Nor does it provide a destination ranking or comparative evidence that a short treatment stay produces a better outcome. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cross-border-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The practical setting is part of access",
        "content": "Research with Afghan immigrants and refugees with disabilities in Iran identified barriers extending beyond the treatment room, including service costs, limited insurance, transport and accommodation expenses, and restricted access in remote areas. This population differs from short-stay medical travelers, so the findings cannot be transferred directly to tourism. They nevertheless demonstrate why the surrounding journey and living arrangements matter when describing access to rehabilitation. [s3]\n\nA stronger travel-specific account requires verified information about admission arrangements, accessible accommodation and transport, language support, and continuity after departure. The present evidence explains rehabilitation's purpose and documented system concerns; it does not establish a verified international rehabilitation itinerary.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
